---
import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { repoUrl, siteBase } from '@/lib/buildprints';

const submitIssueUrl = `${repoUrl}/issues/new?title=${encodeURIComponent('[publish] owner/repo/path')}&body=${encodeURIComponent(`Publish a Buildprint from GitHub

Source repository URL:
Buildprint path inside repo, if not root:
Entrypoint file, if not BUILDPRINT.md:
Title:
Short description:
Creator / maintainer profile:
License:
Tags:

What should agents use this to build or operate?

Registry facts you expect:
- Source repo/branch/path:
- Start or copy instructions:
- Files that should be listed:
- Optional public run/artifact links:

Checks before submission:
- [ ] Repo is public or reviewer-accessible
- [ ] Entrypoint file exists
- [ ] No secrets, tokens, private customer data, or env values are included
- [ ] License/ownership is clear
- [ ] Links resolve
- [ ] Buildprint does not require arbitrary command execution during registry import

Notes for reviewer:

If this came from an existing repo, Mapper OS can help create a reusable packet:
${siteBase}/buildprints/buildprint-mapper-os/
`)}`;
---
<BaseLayout title="Publish from GitHub — Agent Buildprint" description="Publish a Buildprint to the registry from a GitHub repository path.">
  <main class="publish-page">
    <section class="publish-hero">
      <div class="container publish-hero-grid">
        <div>
          <a class="artifact-back" href="/buildprints/">← Registry</a>
          <p class="registry-v2-kicker">Publish</p>
          <h1>Publish a Buildprint from GitHub.</h1>
          <p class="registry-v2-lede">The registry should not own your method. It imports a source repo/path, checks that files are fetchable, and presents factual source/use/files/run information. Today publishing is reviewed through GitHub while the full OAuth flow is being wired.</p>
          <div class="registry-v2-actions">
            <a class="registry-v2-primary" href={submitIssueUrl}>Open GitHub publish request</a>
            <a href="/buildprints/buildprint-mapper-os/">Use Mapper OS</a>
            <a href={repoUrl}>Registry source</a>
          </div>
        </div>
        <aside class="artifact-use-panel">
          <div class="artifact-panel-head"><span>Publication contract</span><b>GitHub-backed</b></div>
          <dl class="artifact-quick-facts">
            <div><dt>Input</dt><dd>repo + path</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Structure</dt><dd>free-form</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Import</dt><dd>file fetch</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Runs</dt><dd>registry-side</dd></div>
          </dl>
        </aside>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="container artifact-layout publish-layout">
      <aside class="artifact-sidebar">
        <nav>
          <a href="#current">Current path</a>
          <a href="#requirements">Requirements</a>
          <a href="#not-required">Not required</a>
          <a href="#future">Full product</a>
        </nav>
        <div class="artifact-side-card"><span>Status</span><strong>Review queue</strong><p>GitHub issue review is the honest current publishing path. No fake app auth is shown.</p></div>
      </aside>

      <div class="artifact-content">
        <section class="artifact-section" id="current">
          <div class="artifact-section-title"><span>Current path</span><h2>Reviewed GitHub import</h2></div>
          <div class="artifact-card artifact-use-grid">
            <div>
              <h3>What you provide</h3>
              <ul>
                <li>GitHub repository URL</li>
                <li>Path to the Buildprint folder or entrypoint</li>
                <li>Title, description, creator, license, tags</li>
                <li>Optional public run/artifact links</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
            <div>
              <h3>What the registry checks</h3>
              <ul>
                <li>Repository/path is fetchable from GitHub</li>
                <li>Entrypoint and listed files resolve</li>
                <li>No obvious secrets or private data are included</li>
                <li>Copy/start instructions are explicit</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="artifact-section" id="requirements">
          <div class="artifact-section-title"><span>Requirements</span><h2>Minimal metadata, not methodology</h2></div>
          <div class="artifact-card artifact-source-grid">
            <div><span>Entrypoint</span><strong>Any clear start file</strong></div>
            <div><span>Source</span><strong>GitHub repo/path</strong></div>
            <div><span>Identity</span><strong>Maintainer-owned repo</strong></div>
            <div><span>Files</span><strong>Fetchable list</strong></div>
            <div><span>Usage</span><strong>Copy/run instructions</strong></div>
            <div><span>License</span><strong>Explicit</strong></div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="artifact-section" id="not-required">
          <div class="artifact-section-title"><span>Not required</span><h2>Your Buildprint can have its own shape</h2></div>
          <div class="artifact-card artifact-prose">
            <p>The registry should not force `PLAN.md`, `CONTRACTS.md`, evidence ledgers, phase folders, or a single Mapper OS packet spine. Those can exist when useful, but publication only needs enough metadata for agents and humans to find the source, start correctly, inspect files, and review execution facts.</p>
            <div class="artifact-empty-run">
              <h3>Runs live outside the packet.</h3>
              <p>Execution proof belongs on registry run pages with command, runtime, status, logs/checks, and artifacts — not as mandatory author-side ceremony inside every Buildprint.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="artifact-section" id="future">
          <div class="artifact-section-title"><span>Full product</span><h2>What the app flow will become</h2></div>
          <div class="artifact-card artifact-read-order">
            <ol>
              <li><strong>Connect GitHub</strong><small>OAuth session with state validation, secure cookies, CSRF, and token secrecy.</small></li>
              <li><strong>Select repo/path</strong><small>GitHub-only URL allowlist, repo/file limits, markdown sanitization.</small></li>
              <li><strong>Preview import</strong><small>Entrypoint, file tree, copy command, sync facts, and actionable errors.</small></li>
              <li><strong>Publish and sync</strong><small>Webhook signature validation, last-good version preservation, audit log, unpublish controls.</small></li>
              <li><strong>Add runs</strong><small>Factual run records from GitHub Actions/manual artifacts first, hosted runner later.</small></li>
            </ol>
          </div>
        </section>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
